# nurbs_patch.cam — the general NURBS patch fixture (DECISIONS.md "the general
# NURBS patch…", slice 1). The ANALYTIC camp-3 path: the same raster drop-cutter
# contract as mesh.cam — serpentine XY passes, ball centre on the upper envelope,
# stepover as the one isotropic sample spacing — but the envelope is computed
# against the exact tensor-product b-spline surface, so the mesh's chord error
# term is gone; only the raster's own sampling term remains (camp 3's charter).
#
# The surface is a hand-checkable bicubic Bézier cap (see cap.patch): a 24x24
# sheet whose boundary lies at z=0 and whose apex sits at (12, 12, 9) exactly.
# With the placement below and a dia-8 ball (r=4) the raster grid runs
# (14..46)^2 at step 2 (17x17 samples), and the apex lands exactly on the sample
# at (30,30): the normal is vertical there, so centre Z = 9 + 4 = 13.0. Grid
# corners are beyond the ball's reach and clamp to the floor: Z = 0 + 4 = 4.0.
units mm
feed 600
plunge 200
rapid_z 5
clearance_z 30
spindle 4000

stock Box(w=60, l=60, h=15, x=0, y=0, z=0)

tool T9 Ballmill(dia=8.0)

# Patch control points are mm by definition (DECISIONS.md); x/y/z place the
# sheet in stock coords — the net spans (18..42)^2, apex at (30,30,9).
feature Dome NurbsPatch(file="cap.patch", x=18, y=18, z=0)

# stepover is both the Y row spacing and the X sample spacing — the same
# isotropic error budget as the mesh raster (parse::derive_mesh_sample_step,
# one home shared deliberately).
run Copy(tool=T9, surface=Dome, stepover=2.0)
